From: jbaker@halcyon.com (James Baker)
Date: Tue, 30 Aug 1994 18:09:44 -0800
Subject: [#94-8-307] Flickering light bulbs

  There has been some discussion of flickering light bulbs recently on the
list.  

  For what it's worth I measured flicker level on a variety of incandesent
bulbs as part of some tests for the design of a video conferencing system.
I found bulbs with about 4% flicker and bulbs with almost zero flicker. The
4% flicker bulbs appear to go completely dark immeadiately when turned off.
Some quartz bulbs slowly go dark over one second or more. These had no
measureable flicker at a resolution of 0.25%.

  The charastic curve falls from 100% to minimum more or less linearly over
about 8 milliseconds and returns to 100% in 200 - 500 microseconds.
Flourescent, and HID lamps such as sodium or metal halide have much greater
flicker but I didn't measure those. The spectrum I measured centered in the
blue.

  I invite anyone who wants more info to contact me.



