and bruceab@teleport.com (Bruce Baugh), the mildest of Netizens, replied:
: Tell me, Bill. Do you know anything about geology? And if not, why are you
: are participating in this exchange?
: 
: There are no suburbs over subduction zones. There aren't any rural
: settlements or tribes over them, either. Let me explain simply to you.
: 
: The surface of the Earth isn't one big solid mass. It's broken up into
: twenty or so "plates", chunks of rock that float around on top of the hot
: stuff below. You may not know that it is very hot indeed inside the Earth,
: so hot that at its core, metals like iron and nickel melt. "Magma" is the
: name given to the melted rock that we find below the surface, a name given
: by scientists who study the Earth and its composition. We call these
: scientists "geologists". Magma is the neat stuff you can see in any National
: Geographic special about volcanoes, that comes up flowing like water until
: it cools down enough to harden. Very impressive.
: 
: The plates I mentioned above bump into each other, and there are different
: kinds of boundaries. Sometimes they slide past each other; this makes
: earthquakes, when they stick for a while and then release in a sudden slip.
: Sometimes they hit head-on; this makes mountains, when the stuff on their
: edges gets pushed up. And sometimes one drifts on top of another. One of the
: plates gets pushed down, bending as it goes. Eventually that descending edge
: gets all heated up and melts, becoming magma again. 
: 
: The area where one plate is sinking under another (that's the same thing as
: one drifting on top of another, but seen from the other direction) is called
: a "subduction zone", for reasons a good dictionary can tell you.
: (Dictionaries are books that consist entirely of definitions of words, big
: and small. They are very handy. And when general dictionaries can't help
: you, with technical terms of different kinds, there are specialized
: dictionaries for various fields. They are handy, too.) What's happening
: here, from the point of view of someone on the surface, is that land is
: getting eaten up. Slowly - but noticeably, over time - land goes away. 
: And it usually doesn't go quietly. Earthquakes often occur around subduction
: zones, just as they do at other kinds of plate boundaries. It's not stable.
: So people generally don't live there.
: 
: Most subduction zones, in fact, occur deep under water. This makes sense,
: when you stop to think about it. The descending plate is bending down, and
: the plate moving over it is riding up, and this creates a nice deep gap
: suitable for holding a lot of water. In many cases there is a deep ocean
: trough right next to high mountains. Do you know what an atlas is? It's a
: book that has many maps. Different atlases have different ranges of
: subjects; find a good atlas of the world, and look at the western coast of
: South America (that's the big sort of comma-shaped continent directly
: "below" us when north is at the top). If the atlas shows water depths as
: well as land heights, you can see that there's one of those troughs off the
: west coast, and then the Andes mountains on the lands. That's a subduction
: zone. But notice that more people live in the mountains than in the trough.
: The mountains shake from time to time, and have other problems, but at least
: people can breathe there.
: 
: It's not really likely that we'll have the suburb/ghetto concern about
: subduction zones until we have a lot of people living in underwater
: communities. And, SEAQUEST DSV to the contrary, that's probably not going to
: happen real soon. (Darwin is usually just a robot rather than a dolphin,
: too. Life is full of disappointments.)
: 
